  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry weather will continue today through most of Wednesday, with\nwarming as we get on the back side of high pressure. That\nsurface high is in charge currently, with radiational cooling\nyielding a fairly chilly start to the day with upper 40s-mid 50s\naround dawn. Ridging is getting underway aloft, but will take\nlonger at the surface as the high slowly edges east and thus\ndewpoints will still be comfortable this afternoon. Sun will be\nmixed with scattered mid-to-high level clouds, but will still \nmanage to help temperatures reach well into the 70s; a few lower\nelevation locations even near 80.\n\nThe high exits more decidedly overnight, making way for\nincreasing southerly to southwesterly warm air advection. Thus\nit will not be quite as cool tonight with lows of mid 50s-lower\n60s. Dewpoints will also be noticeably on their way up, reaching\nmid-upper 60s to cause a muggy feel. Temperatures have also\ntrended a touch higher in latest guidance since we will spend\nall day in the warm sector of the incoming system with at least\npartial sunshine. Highs of upper 70s-mid 80s could easily feel\nanother few degrees higher given the humidity. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nCanadian low pressure will trail a cold front through our region\nlate Wednesday into Thursday. With models trending just a touch\nwarmer ahead of the front and thus a little more instability, \nthere has been a small uptick in potential for thunder Wednesday\nafternoon at least to our west. Ensemble probabilities carry\nthis into the Finger Lakes Wednesday evening, with potential for\na few to several hundred J/kg of Convective Available Potential\nEnergy (CAPE) along with decent 30-40 knots of shear in much of\nthe lowest 6km. However, nocturnal timing for the front itself\nleads to doubt for how far southeast the thunder can manage to\nreach. Regardless, given the amount of shear, a conditional\npotential for a few gusty cells has to be considered and indeed\nthe Storm Prediction Center recently introduced a Marginal Risk\nof Severe Thunderstorms for roughly the Twin Tiers northward,\ncentered over the Finger Lakes.\n\nPrecipitable Water Values will pool along the approaching front\nto beyond 1.75 inches across the Central to Eastern Great \nLakes. The front looks to be fairly progressive as it moves \nthrough so training thunderstorm threat is lowering. That said,\nheavier rainfall rates in some thunderstorms could lead to brief\nlocalized urban/poor drainage water issues if occurring over a \ntown or city Wednesday evening, with northern Finger Lakes to NY\nThruway Corridor a little more prone to that compared to areas\nsouth. Frontal progress will slow somewhat on Thursday, being so\nfar from the parent low and also starting to get more flow-\nparallel. This will keep a chance of showers and perhaps thunder\ngoing mainly south of the Twin Tiers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nHigh pressure a brief shot of cool air advection should yield\na window of Fall-ish weather Thursday night into the first part\nof the weekend, similar to what we recently experienced.\nHowever, with a fast moving zonal pattern aloft, the high will\npass quickly to allow warming to return over the weekend.\nForecast temperatures of 70s to near 80 are figured for Saturday\nand Sunday. That said, moisture will remain quite limited, and \nthus passing yet-to-be-resolved waves embedded in the flow do \nnot have very high odds of producing rain. So for now, chances \nfor showers Saturday night onward are still only in the 20-40 \npercent range.",
